Nineteenth system tracing expansion and PTY retirement (2026-10-13)
=================================================================

Scope: the older terminal/device subsystems reviewed this round. Age alone
is not evidence of replacement. This is not a whole-tree retirement audit.

Replacement decisions
---------------------
The former pty(4) manual identifies the BSD fixed-name driver as compatibility
for older C libraries, replaced by pts(4). Remove sys/dev/pty/pty.c, its module,
configuration/build entries, manual, obsolete legacy-specific stress tests and
kerneldoc entry. Remove PTS_EXTERNAL and pts_alloc_external from the core/header,
including its now-unused pts:::alloc-external contract. Keep POSIX PTYs.

/dev/ptmx was implemented inside the obsolete module but is still required by
Linux emulation and modern clone-device callers. Move its existing fdopen
semantics into the always-built pts core, create the clone node during pts
initialization, and make pts_alloc private. Linux startup and loader/release
configuration no longer load pty.ko. The ordinary /dev/pts/N slave path and
posix_openpt/openpty remain. Update manual references and package obsolete-file
entries. The vnode fork/exit stress test now allocates modern PTYs. Historical
papers and historical trace inventories are retained as historical records.

Compatibility change: applications requiring /dev/ptyXX and /dev/ttyXX naming
must migrate; those names and kern.tty_pty_warningcnt are removed. The old
module must not be loaded with the new kernel. UPDATING describes migration.
No installed module, boot configuration or running kernel was changed here.

Retain tty (terminal discipline/session semantics), uart (physical serial
controllers), lpt (parallel hardware), snp (capture from an already-existing
TTY), and nmdm (paired virtual serial ports with modem/rate behavior). No
functionally complete replacement for those interfaces was found in the
reviewed tree. USB serial and printer drivers address different buses, and
new PTY allocation does not replace physical or paired null-modem interfaces.

New tracing contracts
---------------------
13 new contracts at 21 source sites in six source files. Four profiles bring
the base installation catalog to 442. One obsolete contract was removed,
for a net increase of 12 contracts. All new selectors are sdt:::NAME.

Every new call excludes cold initialization. There are no probes in UART
console getc/putc/grab/poll paths, raw interrupt filters, per-character UART
or nmdm loops, or panic handlers. No new allocation or locking is added.
Arguments are identities, scalar results and configuration, never terminal
characters, credentials, paths or packet contents. Existing locking protects
operational lifetimes; enabled tracing can extend existing lock hold times.

Arguments in order:

tty-sleep-start: tty, kind (1=ordinary; 2=timed; 3=proctree-aware).
tty-sleep-done: tty, kind, raw condition-variable return.
Pairs surround actual sleeps. Kind 3 includes reacquiring the process-tree
lock and TTY lock. Done is before gone/revoke remapping to ENXIO/ERESTART;
it is not necessarily the error returned by tty_wait to its caller.

pts-master-wait-start: unit, direction (1=master read; 2=master write).
pts-master-wait-done: unit, direction, raw condition-variable return.
Only actual waits are paired; immediate/nonblocking operations are excluded.
pts-allocation-limit: cause (1=RACCT_NPTS; 2=RLIMIT_NPTS accounting;
3=no available unit number). Existing EAGAIN return/rollback is unchanged.
This is resource-limit pressure, not proof of global OOM.
pts-ptmx-open: fdopen flags, allocation return. It covers clone-device opens;
posix_openpt keeps its existing provider events. Failed clone allocation may
also emit the causal limit event. No clone probe runs during initialization.

snp-read-wait-start/done: snoop identity (done adds raw wake return).
No captured terminal data is exposed. A successful wake can still lead to
EIO if the terminal disappeared; done does not mean a read returned bytes.

lpt-bus-request-start: device, PPB_* request flags.
lpt-bus-request-done: device, ppb_request_bus return.
This times a bus acquisition attempt, including contention when allowed to
wait. PPB_DONTWAIT calls may return immediately. The already-owned shortcut
is excluded, as are cold boot probing/attach calls. It is not byte-I/O latency.

uart-parameter-start: softc, requested output baud rate.
uart-parameter-done: softc, raw UART_PARAM return before mapping to EINVAL.
Only the TTY parameter callback's hardware-programming operation is paired;
pre-validation failures and the zero-baud hangup branch are excluded. A done
success does not include subsequent flow-control changes. Console callbacks
and UART interrupt servicing are untouched.

nmdm-rate-configured: endpoint, callout interval ticks, scaled credits.
Emits when setting a simulated rate for each endpoint. Credits use the
existing QS fixed-point scale, not unscaled baud. Does not emit on every
transmitted character, timeout tick, or disabled/zero-rate short circuit.

Profiles
--------
terminal-waits: TTY sleep latency and raw results by kind.
pts-pressure-waits: modern PTY master waits, limit causes and ptmx results.
legacy-device-waits: snp waits and lpt bus acquisition latency/results.
serial-configuration: UART parameter latency/results and nmdm rate intervals.
All profiles use scalar aggregates without dereferencing pointers. Pairs use
PID/TID/depth keys and clear matched state. Start before the workload; lost
events invalidate pairing. Elapsed times include scheduling and lock reacquisition.

Validation and limits
---------------------
Artifacts: /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-nineteenth-validation.
Native module links: snp, lpt, uart and nmdm, using all CPUs and /usr/obj.
Twelve native source compiles with tracing hooks enabled/disabled passed.
Five instrumentation-only sources retain identical no-hooks .text after
normalizing diagnostic locations. tty_pts.c intentionally changes semantics
and layout for retirement and is excluded from that equivalence claim.
Eight offline profile compiles passed, including PID/duration filtering;
selectors, argument bounds, installed profile entries and object NOP sites
are checked. All four linked driver modules retain their new probe sites.
The prepared GENERIC-NODEBUG kernel links, retaining all six new core probe
contracts. ptmx_fdopen is linked; pts_alloc_external, pty_clone and pty_modevent
are absent from the linked kernel.

Four rc startup tests passed against source with mocked module loaders and
mounts. PTY regression tests compile; fionread and ptmx_core pass on the running
host as ABI baseline checks, not validation of the rebuilt kernel. The new
legacy_names_absent case must run after booting the new kernel and has not
been executed here. A fresh boot, native/ Linux PTY runtime tests, and actual
hardware tests remain outstanding; no boot/install is authorized or performed.
/dev/dtrace is unavailable, so live firing/overhead remain unqualified.

Maintained scripts: coverage-nineteenth-{modules,compile,check,codegen,linked,
core-linked}.py. Build mapping and contracts are the adjacent JSON files.
static-census.py performs the separate reproducible source-contract comparison
against a pinned vanilla FreeBSD commit; see PROBE-COMPARISON-NINETEENTH for
the exact metric, exclusions, totals and limits on the 3x claim.
